        "problem": "If $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061a=-3,$ then $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(-a)=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13942972/assets/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph about the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(-x)=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(-a) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061a \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
